WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:06.279
When Kate Flannery exited The Office for the
last time, she did so having created and embodied

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:09.840
Meredith, one of the funniest secondary characters
on television.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:14.400
But just what has Flannery been up to since
leaving Meredith Palmer behind?

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.240
After nine long seasons, The Office left the
air on a high note, with the iconic wedding

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:25.240
of two main characters, Dwight and Angela,
a send-off that sat well with Flannery.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:28.420
Speaking to the blog How to Be a Readhead,
she explained:

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.260
"I am so proud of the ending of The Office.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.600
I felt that everything was wrapped up so nicely.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.940
It was sad and funny and thoughtful and everything
you want in a finale…I am so sad, but I

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:40.020
am so proud.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:43.780
It's a great feeling to be a part of such
a respected and honored project."

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:48.839
The end of the show also made Flannery feel
nostalgic and reflective, which she discussed

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:50.880
in an essay she wrote for Huffington Post.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:51.560
She wrote:

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.660
"To be a part of an ensemble on TV is a gift.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.400
I stopped counting lines.

00:00:56.409 --> 00:01:00.780
I was disappointed at first when my lines
would get cut but it dawned on me, I don't

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:01.829
need to be the meat.

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:03.440
I don't need to be the side dish.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:04.900
I can be the onions.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.360
It's good to be the onions."

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.240
"Don't fall in love with me, kid."

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:15.479
When it came to summing up The Office's appeal
to fans and audiences around the world, Flannery

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:18.160
put it about as succinctly as you can.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:18.660
She wrote:

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.620
"Abraham Lincoln said most people are as happy
as they make up their minds to be...This show

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:29.220
made its mind up to have heart and soul and
treat the audience like it had a brain."

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:35.820
Nowadays, Flannery gets to flex her musical
muscles in her comedy lounge act the Lampshades,

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.960
in which she is joined by fellow actor actor
Scot Robinson.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:40.720
Flannery describes the act as:

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:46.260
"...a male/ female duo fresh from the cruise
ship circuit, spinning pop and rock into comedy

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:50.300
gold as they work out the kinks in their amusingly
tortured relationship."

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:55.659
The duo plays regularly, mostly in southern
California comedy clubs, and for a number

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:58.880
of years they had a standing gig at the IO
Theater in Hollywood.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.160
Flannery says:

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.400
"It's been musically interesting.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:07.240
We will gig out and host stand-up nights and
we've done a lot of charity events and some

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:08.100
private parties.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:12.080
It's a very portable show, so it's been really,
really great for us.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:16.400
We've gotten around a lot doing it and we've
met a lot of people because of it."

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.160
In addition to the Lampshades, Flannery also
sings and performs with Glee star Jane Lynch

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:25.420
in Lynch's comedy cabaret show See Jane Sing.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.940
The pair's working relationship goes back
to the early days of a stage show called The

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:35.159
Real Live Brady Bunch, when Flannery served
as Lynch's understudy and acted in several

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.240
of the play's non-Brady roles.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:43.080
And while much of See Jane Sing seems to be
improvised, Lynch says this simply isn't the

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:43.580
case.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.560
"I like to script every damn moment.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.880
I build a cage but then I bounce around freely
within the cage."

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.200
Flannery herself, however, does seem to keep
things fresh.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:57.170
"And she's loosey-goosey.

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:02.050
Yeah, nothing's the same twice for her, so,
and that keeps me alive and going, 'What is

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:03.040
she gonna do now?'"

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:08.720
A film Variety called a "thoroughly ingratiating,
touchingly heartfelt comedy," 4th Man Out

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:14.060
tells the story of Adam, a small-town car
mechanic who, after a night of drinking, comes

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:18.240
out to his three lifelong friends, who are
surprised to learn he is gay.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.920
The bulk of the film finds them attempting
to maintain the equilibrium in their friendship,

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.480
while helping Adam find a relationship of
his own.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:30.980
Flannery plays Adam's mother, Karen, and says
the film is a product of an increasingly tolerant

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:32.500
audience and society.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.880
In an interview with the film's cast, she
told Hollywood Chicago:

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:41.470
"The world has changed so much, and younger
kids have so much less judgement about everything...they

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:43.390
don't care to draw lines between people.

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:48.240
I think that is fantastic, and this movie
is the evolution of coming out in America.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:51.440
This is an amazing story that represents that
change."

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:54.740
Parker Young, who plays one of Adam's friends
in the film, agreed.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:55.380
He said:

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:59.900
"Our generation is more aware of what we think
about it as individuals, and we're not listening

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:02.040
to negative backlash when we don't agree with
it."

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:07.560
Flannery maintains a robust presence on Twitter
and Instagram, tweeting and posting photos

00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:12.420
of a number of things, including her exploits
offstage, her time spent with friends and

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.600
other celebrities, and her slate of current
and upcoming projects.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:21.060
She is also fond of retweeting fans' posts
and a seemingly endless stream of memes, both

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:24.520
of The Office in general, and of her character
Meredith specifically.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.490
She doesn't always feel comfortable in the
virtual realm, though.

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:32.900
In an interview with Google, Flannery noted
that engaging with fans online can often be

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:33.910
pretty difficult.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:37.780
"I have to be a little more self-concious
than normally would be, and I just sort of

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:41.960
have to figure out, like, 'Okay, what...what
am I really...who needs to know what?'"

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:46.139
In fact, she sees a problem with social media
personalities in general.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.760
"For me I just feel like, sometimes, there's
such an ego-centric thing that happens with

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:52.270
the sense of celebrity, even at the smallest
level."

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:57.780
In the years since The Office closed for good,
Flannery has filled her onscreen time with

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.940
a range of different projects, most of them
independently produced films and television

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:02.440
shows.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:08.360
She plays the role of nosy next-door neighbor
Mrs. Jenkins in Stuck, a 2017 movie in which

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:12.720
the main character, Darby, is sentenced to
a month of house arrest in the same house

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.100
as her ex-boyfriend and his new fiancee.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:20.900
Flannery also took a part in Fishbowl, California,
a film about a drunk, chronically ill widow

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.360
who takes in an aimless twentysomething.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:29.560
Finally, Flannery plays Principal Saperstein
in the Hulu comedy series All Night, which

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:31.600
Deadline describes as centering:

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:36.620
"...on an overnight, lock-in graduation party
in which a group of new grads will do whatever

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:39.560
it takes to make their remaining high school
dreams come true."

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:45.690
While none of these projects have quite constituted
an Office-sized hit, each has benefited hugely

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:47.980
from Flannery's wit and comedic chops.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:51.800
And considering the current pace at which
she's working, it's safe to assume that more

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:55.180
shows and movies will be added to the list
in due course.

